              "logprobs": [],
              "text": "Below are drop‑in replacements that track current Node.js best practices (as of 2024–2025). I assume Node.js 22 LTS or newer (recommended for modern Web APIs). Where relevant, I note alternatives and any behavior differences you’ll want to account for.\n\n1) File operations: Bun.file(...) and Bun.write(...)\n- Best replacement\n  - Reading, writing, existence: node:fs/promises (readFile, writeFile, access) and node:fs (constants). ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.16.0/docs/api/fs.html?utm_source=openai))\n  - If you specifically relied on Bun.file being Blob‑like: fs.openAsBlob(path) (now stable in Node 22.17+ / Node 24+) for Blob/stream/text/json helpers. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - There’s no fs.promises.exists; use fs.promises.access or fs.stat wrapped in a try/catch. ([masteringjs.io](https://masteringjs.io/tutorials/node/check-if-file-exists?utm_source=openai))\n  - writeFile handles strings, Buffers, typed arrays, and (in modern Node) also accepts AsyncIterable/Stream, but not Response directly. If you have a Web Response, use await response.arrayBuffer() or response.body with stream APIs. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.10.0/docs/api/fs.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Example\n```ts\nimport {readFile, writeFile, access} from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport {constants as fsc} from 'node:fs';\nimport {openAsBlob} from 'node:fs'; // only if you need Blob-like\n\nasync function exists(path: string) {\n  try { await access(path, fsc.F_OK); return true; }\n  catch { return false; }\n}\n\nconst filepath = 'file.txt';\n\nif (await exists(filepath)) {\n  const content = await readFile(filepath, 'utf8');\n  await writeFile('copy.txt', content);\n}\n\n// Blob-like (optional)\nconst blob = await openAsBlob(filepath);\nconst text = await blob.text();\n```\n\n2) Process spawning: Bun.spawn(...)\n- Best replacement\n  - execa (npm) for simple, cross‑platform, promise‑based process execution; supports template tag, piping, streaming, env/cwd, etc. It’s the most ergonomic Bun.spawn analogue for CLIs. ([github.com](https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa?utm_source=openai))\n  - Native: node:child_process.spawn is fine if you want zero deps; you’ll do your own buffering/stream handling. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest/api/child_process.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - Bun.spawn’s proc.exited is a Promise; execa returns a Promise that resolves with {stdout, stderr, exitCode}. Node’s spawn returns an EventEmitter, not a Promise. ([github.com](https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa?utm_source=openai))\n- Example (execa)\n```ts\nimport {execa} from 'execa';\n\nconst {stdout, exitCode} = await execa({cwd: '/path', env: {...process.env}})`git status`;\nconsole.log(exitCode, stdout);\n```\n\n3) Glob pattern matching: new Bun.Glob(...).scan() and .match()\n- Best replacement\n  - glob (npm; maintained by @isaacs). It supports async iteration, streams, and a Glob class with for‑await scanning, very close to Bun.Glob ergonomics. For matching a single path against a pattern, use minimatch. ([github.com](https://github.com/isaacs/node-glob))\n- Gotchas\n  - Pattern semantics are shell‑style (forward slashes recommended across platforms). For single‑string “does this path match?” checks, glob’s walk isn’t needed—use minimatch(pattern, path). ([github.com](https://github.com/isaacs/node-glob))\n- Example\n```ts\nimport {Glob} from 'glob';\nimport {minimatch} from 'minimatch';\n\nconst g = new Glob('**/*', {cwd: dir});\nfor await (const file of g) {\n  // ...\n}\n\nif (minimatch(file, pattern)) {\n  // matched\n}\n```\n\n4) Finding executables: Bun.which(...)\n- Best replacement\n  - which (npm). Supports async, sync, and custom PATH. ([npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/which?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - Returns null if not found when you pass {nothrow: true}; otherwise it throws. This maps well to Bun.which returning null when not found. ([npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/which?utm_source=openai))\n- Example\n```ts\nimport which from 'which';\n\nconst gofmt = await which('gofmt', {nothrow: true});\nif (gofmt) { /* ... */ }\n\nconst gopls = await which('gopls', {path: customPath, nothrow: true});\n```\n\n5) HTTP server: Bun.serve(...) with Hono\n- Best replacement\n  - @hono/node-server’s serve() for Hono on Node.js. Same app.fetch shape; options for port, hostname, HTTPS, etc. ([honojs.com](https://www.honojs.com/docs/getting-started/nodejs?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - Node has several timeouts (requestTimeout, headersTimeout, keepAliveTimeout) with different defaults than Bun. If you were using idleTimeout: 0 in Bun to disable idling, explicitly tune Node’s server timeouts (e.g., set requestTimeout to 0 only if you understand the DoS trade‑offs). With @hono/node-server, you can supply createServer + serverOptions and then adjust the returned http.Server if needed. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/http.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Example (Hono on Node)\n```ts\nimport {Hono} from 'hono';\nimport {serve} from '@hono/node-server';\n\nconst app = new Hono();\napp.get('/', c => c.text('Hello'));\n\nconst server = serve({fetch: app.fetch, port: opts.port, hostname: opts.hostname});\n// server is a Node http.Server; you can tweak timeouts if necessary:\n// server.requestTimeout = 0; // beware DoS implications\n```\n\n6) Shell template tag: import { $ } from 'bun'\n- Best replacement\n  - execa’s $ tag for template‑literal commands, with safe interpolation and great piping ergonomics. If you prefer a scripting‑oriented tool with .text(), consider zx; but for a library/CLI, execa keeps dependencies focused. ([github.com](https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - In execa, the result is an object with stdout rather than a .text() method (zx has .text()). Both allow $`mkdir -p ${dir}` and $`git describe --tags`. ([github.com](https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa?utm_source=openai))\n- Example (execa)\n```ts\nimport {$} from 'execa';\n\nawait $`git describe --tags`;\nawait $`mkdir -p ${dir}`;\nconst {stdout} = await $`command`;\n```\n\n7) Stream utility: readableStreamToText from \"bun\"\n- Best replacement\n  - node:stream/consumers text(stream). Works with both Node streams and WHATWG ReadableStream. Alternatively: new Response(webReadable).text() when you already have Web streams. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/webstreams.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - child_process.spawn in Node returns Node streams (not Web streams). consumers.text() accepts either, so you don’t need to convert. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/webstreams.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Example\n```ts\nimport {text as streamToText} from 'node:stream/consumers';\nimport {spawn} from 'node:child_process';\n\nconst proc = spawn('git', ['status'], {cwd: '/path', stdio: ['inherit','pipe','pipe']});\nconst output = await streamToText(proc.stdout!);\n```\n\n8) Module resolution: Bun.resolve(specifier, parent)\n- Best replacement\n  - ESM: import.meta.resolve(specifier) when resolving relative to the current module (stable in Node 22+). If you need a different base (“parent”), either:\n    - Use module.createRequire(baseFile).resolve(specifier) to resolve like require.resolve from an arbitrary directory, or\n    - Use the import-meta-resolve package to resolve with a custom parent URL in pure ESM. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.0.1/docs/api/module.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - Node’s import.meta.resolve’s second parent argument is still behind a flag; prefer createRequire(...) or import-meta-resolve when you must resolve from a custom base. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.11.0/docs/api/esm.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Examples\nESM (relative to current module):\n```ts\nconst url = await import.meta.resolve('typescript/lib/tsserver.js');\n```\nResolve from an arbitrary cwd via createRequire:\n```ts\nimport {createRequire} from 'node:module';\nimport path from 'node:path';\n\nconst requireFromCwd = createRequire(path.join(app.path.cwd, 'noop.js'));\nconst tsserver = requireFromCwd.resolve('typescript/lib/tsserver.js');\n```\nOr with import-meta-resolve:\n```ts\nimport {resolve as resolveWithParent} from 'import-meta-resolve';\nimport {pathToFileURL} from 'node:url';\nconst tsserverURL = await resolveWithParent(\n  'typescript/lib/tsserver.js',\n  pathToFileURL(app.path.cwd + '/').href\n);\n```\n\n9) URL utilities: Bun.fileURLToPath(url)\n- Best replacement\n  - url.fileURLToPath from node:url. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v24.0.1/docs/api/url.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Gotchas\n  - Provide a file: URL; you’ll get a platform‑specific path (handles percent‑decoding properly). ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v18.20.8/docs/api/url.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Example\n```ts\nimport {fileURLToPath} from 'node:url';\nconst path = fileURLToPath(new URL('file:///C:/path/example.txt'));\n```\n\nExtra notes and equivalence tips\n- Web streams and fetch: Node 18+ has global fetch and WHATWG Web Streams powered by undici; response.body is a Web ReadableStream. Convert between Web and Node streams with Readable.fromWeb() when needed. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/fetch?utm_source=openai))\n- HTTP timeouts: If you relied on Bun’s idleTimeout: 0, emulate carefully with server.timeout, server.requestTimeout, and server.keepAliveTimeout. Defaults differ; in Node 18+ requestTimeout default is 300s; server.timeout default is 0 (no socket timeout). ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/http.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nQuick side-by-side replacement snippets\n\n- Bun.file + exists/text/write → fs/promises\n```ts\n// Bun\nconst file = Bun.file(filepath);\nif (await file.exists()) { ... }\nconst content = await file.text();\nawait Bun.write(filepath, content);\n\n// Node\nimport {access, readFile, writeFile} from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport {constants as fsconstants} from 'node:fs';\n\ntry { await access(filepath, fsconstants.F_OK); /* exists */ } catch {}\nconst content = await readFile(filepath, 'utf8');\nawait writeFile(filepath, content);\n```\n\n- Bun.spawn + readableStreamToText → execa (or child_process + consumers)\n```ts\n// Bun\nconst proc = Bun.spawn({ cmd: ['git','status'], cwd: '/path', stdout: 'pipe', stderr: 'pipe', stdin: 'inherit', env: { ...process.env }});\nconst code = await proc.exited;\nconst output = await readableStreamToText(proc.stdout);\n\n// Node (execa)\nimport {execa} from 'execa';\nconst {stdout, exitCode} = await execa({cwd: '/path', env: {...process.env}})`git status`;\n```\n\n- Bun.Glob → glob + minimatch\n```ts\n// scan\nimport {Glob} from 'glob';\nfor await (const file of new Glob('**/*', {cwd: dir})) { /* ... */ }\n\n// match single file\nimport {minimatch} from 'minimatch';\nif (minimatch(file, pattern)) { /* ... */ }\n```\n\n- Bun.which → which\n```ts\nimport which from 'which';\nconst bin = await which('gopls', {path: customPath, nothrow: true});\n```\n\n- Bun.serve (Hono) → @hono/node-server\n```ts\nimport {serve} from '@hono/node-server';\nserve({fetch: app().fetch, port: opts.port, hostname: opts.hostname});\n```\n\n- $ from 'bun' → execa $\n```ts\nimport {$} from 'execa';\nawait $`git describe --tags`;\nawait $`mkdir -p ${dir}`;\nconst {stdout} = await $`command`;\n```\n\n- readableStreamToText → node:stream/consumers\n```ts\nimport {text as streamText} from 'node:stream/consumers';\nconst output = await streamText(proc.stdout!);\n```\n\n- Bun.resolve → createRequire / import.meta.resolve / import-meta-resolve\n```ts\nimport {createRequire} from 'node:module';\nconst requireFrom = createRequire(app.path.cwd + '/noop.js');\nconst resolved = requireFrom.resolve('typescript/lib/tsserver.js');\n```\n\n- Bun.fileURLToPath → node:url\n```ts\nimport {fileURLToPath} from 'node:url';\nconst p = fileURLToPath(url);\n```\n\nSources\n- Node.js docs: fs/promises and fs.openAsBlob; streams consumers; child_process; http timeouts; ESM and module.createRequire; URL utilities. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.10.0/docs/api/fs.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Hono on Node: @hono/node-server usage and options. ([honojs.com](https://www.honojs.com/docs/getting-started/nodejs?utm_source=openai))\n- glob and minimatch packages. ([github.com](https://github.com/isaacs/node-glob))\n- which package. ([npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/which?utm_source=openai))\n- execa documentation (including $ tag, piping). ([github.com](https://github.com/sindresorhus/execa?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you want, I can generate codemods (jscodeshift/TSMorph) to automatically replace these Bun APIs with the Node equivalents across your codebase."
