              "text": "Below is a practical migration plan for opencode’s architecture and build system, with current best practices (Jan 2026) and trade‑offs. I assume you’ll target modern Node (recommend Node 24 LTS today; Node 22 is in Maintenance LTS).\n\nExecutive summary\n- Dev: Use Node’s native TypeScript for simple runs and tsx for day‑to‑day DX (watch, ESM/CJS bridging, tsconfig paths). Keep tsc --noEmit in CI. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/en/learn/typescript/run-natively))\n- Single executable: Prefer Node SEA (Single Executable Applications) plus a proper bundler step to produce one CommonJS bundle. SEA supports embedding “assets” (binary blobs) you can extract at runtime (good Bun.embed analogue). SEA is still marked “active development,” CJS‑only for the embedded entry, and not supported on Alpine/musl; plan per‑platform builds. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html))\n- Alternative to embedding: Ship the Go TUI separately and lazy‑download on first run (esbuild/sharp pattern). Use env‑paths for cache location and a robust downloader; or bin-wrapper if you prefer a well‑trodden helper. ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/getting-started/?utm_source=openai))\n- Bundling: For the TS CLI, use esbuild directly (mature, fast, reliable) to emit a single CJS file that SEA can load. If you later publish libraries, consider tsdown (Rolldown‑based, successor to tsup) instead of tsup (now minimally maintained). ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/content-types/?utm_source=openai))\n- Workspaces: Migrate Bun workspaces to pnpm workspaces. Replace Bun’s catalog: with pnpm.overrides (or npm overrides) at the repo root. Use the workspace: protocol for local linking. ([pnpm.io](https://pnpm.io/9.x/workspaces?utm_source=openai))\n\n1) TypeScript execution for development\nOptions\n- Node’s native TS execution\n  - Node 22.18+ runs .ts files directly for “erasable” TS syntax with no flags: node file.ts. For non‑erasable constructs (enums, namespaces), use --experimental-transform-types. Node ignores tsconfig at runtime; keep tsc --noEmit for type‑checks. Best when your code sticks to erasable syntax. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/en/learn/typescript/run-natively))\n- tsx (recommended for day‑to‑day dev)\n  - Drop‑in replacement for node with watch mode, great ESM/CJS ergonomics, loader mode, and tsconfig paths support. Typical usage: tsx src/index.ts or tsx watch src/index.ts. Works seamlessly with ESM‑first packages (common in the AI SDK ecosystem). ([tsx.is](https://tsx.is/?utm_source=openai))\n- ts-node\n  - Mature, but ESM relies on Node loader hooks and remains more finicky/slower than tsx. Reasonable if you want tight integration with the TypeScript compiler, but not my first pick for a CLI. ([github.com](https://github.com/TypeStrong/ts-node?utm_source=openai))\n\nSuggested dev scripts\n- package.json\n  - \"type\": \"module\" (ESM)\n  - \"dev\": \"tsx watch packages/opencode/src/index.ts\"\n  - Keep \"check\": \"tsc --noEmit\"\n- If you want to run the Go TUI alongside the TS backend in dev:\n  - \"dev:all\": \"concurrently -k \\\"tsx watch packages/opencode/src/index.ts\\\" \\\"go run ./packages/tui/cmd/opencode\\\"\"\n  - Use concurrently for cross‑platform parallel runs. ([npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/concurrently?utm_source=openai))\n\n2) Creating one executable for production\nWhat exists in Node land\n- Node SEA (Single Executable Applications)\n  - Native to Node. You inject a “blob” (your bundled script + optional assets) into a node runtime to create one binary per target OS/arch. It currently runs a single embedded CJS script (not ESM). It supports packaging “assets” retrievable at runtime via node:sea, which is the closest match to Bun’s embeddedFiles. SEA is still marked “Active development.” Not supported on Alpine (musl). Build per target on CI (Linux/glibc, macOS, Windows). ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html))\n- nexe\n  - Long‑running project to pack Node apps; can embed resources via -r and read them via fs.readFile. Useful if you prefer non‑SEA packaging, but SEA is the direction of travel in core. ([github.com](https://github.com/nexe/nexe?utm_source=openai))\n- pkg\n  - vercel/pkg is archived/deprecated; use forks (e.g., tetratelabs/node-pkg) if you must, but I’d prefer SEA. ([github.com](https://github.com/vercel/pkg?utm_source=openai))\n\nCan you embed arbitrary binaries like Bun? Yes—with SEA assets\n- SEA config supports an assets map; use sea.getAsset() or sea.getAssetAsBlob() to retrieve at runtime and write to disk with executable perms—this replaces Bun.embeddedFiles. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nRecommended SEA build flow for opencode\n- Step A: bundle your CLI to a single CommonJS file\n  - esbuild example:\n    - esbuild packages/opencode/src/index.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs --target=node22 --outfile=dist/cli.cjs\n  - esbuild discards types and is robust with ESM/CJS dependencies; we need CJS specifically for SEA entry. ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/content-types/?utm_source=openai))\n- Step B: prepare SEA config (add the Go TUI as an asset)\n  - sea-config.json (per platform when embedding that platform’s TUI):\n    {\n      \"main\": \"dist/cli.cjs\",\n      \"output\": \"dist/sea.blob\",\n      \"useCodeCache\": false,\n      \"useSnapshot\": false,\n      \"assets\": {\n        \"tui-bin\": \"dist/tui/<os-arch>/tui\"  // your prebuilt Go binary\n      }\n    }\n  - Note: for cross‑platform blob generation, keep useCodeCache/useSnapshot false. You inject this blob into a node binary for the matching OS/arch using postject. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Step C: inject and sign\n  - npx postject opencode NODE_SEA_BLOB dist/sea.blob --sentinel-fuse NODE_SEA_FUSE_fce680ab2cc467b6e072b8b5df1996b2\n  - On macOS/Windows, remove and re‑add signature as per docs (then codesign). ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html))\n\nRuntime extraction (replace your Bun.embeddedFiles block)\n```ts\n// tui.ts\nimport { getAssetAsBlob, isSea } from 'node:sea';\nimport { writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport { chmod } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport path from 'node:path';\nimport envPaths from 'env-paths';\n\nexport async function ensureTui(binaryName: string) {\n  if (!isSea()) return null; // when running plain node in dev\n  const blob = await getAssetAsBlob('tui-bin');\n  const cacheDir = path.join(envPaths('opencode').cache, 'tui');\n  const binPath = path.join(cacheDir, binaryName);\n  // ensure dirs exist, then write+chmod\n  await writeFile(binPath, Buffer.from(await blob.arrayBuffer()));\n  if (process.platform !== 'win32') await chmod(binPath, 0o755);\n  return binPath;\n}\n```\nThen spawn the returned path. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nPros/cons of the SEA approach\n- Pros: single file distribution, first‑party tooling, asset embedding.\n- Cons: CJS entry only; per‑OS/arch injection; not for Alpine; “active development” status means you should test thoroughly before large releases. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html))\n\n3) Alternative: ship the Go TUI separately and download on first run\nPattern\n- Keep the Node CLI small; on first launch (or on postinstall), detect OS/arch, download the correct TUI from GitHub Releases, verify checksum, cache in OS‑specific cache dir, and run. This mirrors how esbuild/sharp deliver prebuilt executables. ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/getting-started/?utm_source=openai))\n- You can hand‑roll this with undici + env-paths + tar/zip, or use a helper like bin-wrapper (or its maintained fork @xhmikosr/bin-wrapper). ([npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/env-paths?utm_source=openai))\n\nMinimal “first‑run” downloader\n```ts\nimport { mkdir, writeFile, chmod } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport { createWriteStream } from 'node:fs';\nimport { pipeline } from 'node:stream/promises';\nimport { request } from 'undici';\nimport crypto from 'node:crypto';\nimport path from 'node:path';\nimport envPaths from 'env-paths';\n\nasync function ensureTui(version: string, binName: string) {\n  const cacheDir = path.join(envPaths('opencode').cache, 'tui', version);\n  const exe = path.join(cacheDir, binName);\n  try { await chmod(exe, 0o755); return exe; } catch {}\n  await mkdir(cacheDir, { recursive: true });\n\n  // pick the right asset by process.platform/arch and fetch from GitHub\n  const url = `https://github.com/yourorg/opencode/releases/download/v${version}/tui-${process.platform}-${process.arch}.tar.gz`;\n  const expectedSha256 = lookupChecksum(version, process.platform, process.arch);\n\n  const res = await request(url);\n  const tgzPath = path.join(cacheDir, 'tui.tgz');\n  await pipeline(res.body, createWriteStream(tgzPath));\n\n  // verify checksum, then extract and chmod (use a tar lib or system tar)\n  const buf = await Bun.file(tgzPath).arrayBuffer(); // replace with fs if not using Bun\n  const sha = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(Buffer.from(buf)).digest('hex');\n  if (sha !== expectedSha256) throw new Error('Checksum mismatch');\n  // ...extract tgz to exe...\n  if (process.platform !== 'win32') await chmod(exe, 0o755);\n  return exe;\n}\n```\n\nPros/cons\n- Pros: no SEA/CJS constraints; you can update the TUI independently; smaller npm install footprint for the JS core.\n- Cons: requires network (or a postinstall step); more moving parts; on macOS notarization/quarantine rules apply to downloaded binaries—use a signed/notarized TUI or your Homebrew/AUR routes. Homebrew increasingly enforces signed/notarized casks. ([goreleaser.com](https://goreleaser.com/customization/homebrew_casks/?utm_source=openai))\n\nWhen to choose which\n- You want one file like today: go SEA + assets.\n- You want simpler builds and faster releases: ship TUI separately and bootstrap on first run (or on postinstall), plus Homebrew/AUR for users who prefer their package manager.\n\n4) Build toolchain recommendations\nBundler for the TS CLI (targets SEA and plain Node)\n- esbuild (recommended): stable, very fast, and perfect for bundling your CLI down to one CJS file for SEA. Example:\n  - esbuild packages/opencode/src/index.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs --target=node22 --outfile=dist/cli.cjs\n  - Keep tsc --noEmit for types. ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/content-types/?utm_source=openai))\n- tsup: widely used, but now marked “not actively maintained—consider tsdown.” If you’re starting fresh in 2026, prefer esbuild or tsdown. ([github.com](https://github.com/egoist/tsup?utm_source=openai))\n- tsdown (library‑oriented): a fast Rolldown‑based successor compatible with many tsup options; great for building library outputs with d.ts bundling. For a CLI for SEA, esbuild remains simpler today. ([tsdown.dev](https://tsdown.dev/?utm_source=openai))\n\nGo TUI builds\n- Keep your existing go build flags. Consider automating with GoReleaser to produce per‑OS/arch artifacts (and to generate Homebrew/AUR metadata). GoReleaser also documents signing/notarization flows for macOS (increasingly required for casks). ([goreleaser.com](https://goreleaser.com/customization/archive/?utm_source=openai))\n\nCross‑platform and CI\n- Use a GitHub Actions matrix for {linux-x64, linux-arm64, macos-arm64, macos-x64, win32-x64}. For SEA, inject the blob into the platform’s node binary on that runner; don’t enable code cache/snapshot when producing cross‑platform blobs. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Note SEA platform caveat: not supported on Alpine. Offer tarballs for Alpine or the splitter approach. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.17.1/docs/api/single-executable-applications.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nHomebrew/AUR distribution\n- If you publish a single SEA binary (or separate TUI), GoReleaser can produce casks/taps and handle notarization/signing guides. Homebrew is trending toward requiring signed/notarized casks. ([goreleaser.com](https://goreleaser.com/customization/homebrew_casks/?utm_source=openai))\n\n5) Package manager and monorepo\n- pnpm workspaces\n  - Add pnpm-workspace.yaml at repo root and switch inter‑package deps to \"workspace:*\" (or \"workspace:^\"). This is the closest friction‑free analogue to Bun’s workspaces. ([pnpm.io](https://pnpm.io/9.x/workspaces?utm_source=openai))\n- Bun’s catalog:… → pnpm.overrides or npm overrides\n  - Define version overrides at the root to pin transitive versions repo‑wide. Example (pnpm):\n    \"pnpm\": { \"overrides\": { \"chalk\": \"^5.3.0\", \"left-pad@^1\": \"npm:@myfork/left-pad@^1.3.0\" } }\n  - npm’s overrides offers similar behavior if you standardize on npm. ([pnpm.io](https://pnpm.io/9.x/package_json?utm_source=openai))\n\nCompatibility with ESM and the AI SDK ecosystem\n- Most modern AI SDK packages (e.g., Vercel’s ai) target Node 18+ with ESM ergonomics. For the smoothest interop:\n  - Use \"type\": \"module\".\n  - For dev runs, tsx shields you from ESM/CJS edge cases.\n  - For the SEA entry, bundle to CJS with esbuild, which avoids ESM-in-SEA limitations. ([ai-sdk.dev](https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/getting-started/nodejs?utm_source=openai))\n\nPutting it all together: two concrete release tracks\n\nTrack A — Single binary (SEA + embedded TUI)\n1) Build Go TUI per OS/arch:\n   - CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=${os} GOARCH=${arch} go build -ldflags=\"-s -w\" -o dist/tui/${os}-${arch}/tui ./packages/tui/cmd/opencode\n2) Bundle TS to CJS:\n   - esbuild packages/opencode/src/index.ts --bundle --platform=node --format=cjs --target=node22 --outfile=dist/cli.cjs\n3) Create sea-config.json per OS/arch with \"assets\": {\"tui-bin\": \"dist/tui/<os-arch>/tui\"} and \"useCodeCache\": false.\n4) node --experimental-sea-config sea-config.json\n5) postject the blob into a copy of the node binary; codesign on macOS; sign on Windows. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html))\n\nRuntime: call ensureTui() (the node:sea snippet above) before you spawn the TUI.\n\nTrack B — Split delivery (download TUI on first run)\n1) Publish opencode (JS CLI) to npm; the CLI downloads the TUI per platform on first run into env‑paths('opencode').cache and verifies the checksum; or wire bin-wrapper to do it for you. ship checksums alongside releases. ([npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/%40xhmikosr/bin-wrapper?utm_source=openai))\n2) For Homebrew/AUR, point the formula/PKGBUILD at your per‑platform TUI tarballs and (optionally) your SEA or Node+JS package if you still ship it.\n\nAnswers to your specific questions\n\n1. Best way to run TS in dev?\n- Use tsx for daily work (watch, ESM/CJS smoothing, tsconfig paths). Keep tsc --noEmit in CI. Node’s native TS is great for quick scripts or CI jobs; add --experimental-transform-types only if you use TS constructs that require transforms. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/en/learn/typescript/run-natively))\n\n2. Single executable options in Node.js?\n- Node SEA is the recommended path now; good enough for production with testing. Limitations: CJS entry only; per‑OS/arch injection; not Alpine. It can embed binary assets you can write out at runtime (close to Bun’s embedding). If you prefer userland packagers, nexe works; avoid the archived vercel/pkg unless you adopt a maintained fork. ([nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/api/single-executable-applications.html))\n\n3. Ship TUI separately and download on first run?\n- Yes—this is a common pattern (e.g., esbuild). Use env‑paths to cache and a checksum gate; optionally bin-wrapper for convenience. Pros: simpler build/release, flexible updates, smaller npm package. Cons: first‑run download, macOS notarization considerations. ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/getting-started/?utm_source=openai))\n\n4. Build toolchain (TS portion) and cross‑platform builds?\n- Use esbuild to emit a single CJS bundle for SEA. If you later publish libraries, tsdown is an emerging, fast choice; tsup is moving to maintenance mode. Build the Go TUI per OS/arch; on CI, inject SEA blobs per platform and sign/notarize where required (GoReleaser docs cover macOS notarization). ([esbuild.github.io](https://esbuild.github.io/content-types/?utm_source=openai))\n\n5. Package manager (Bun workspaces with catalog:)\n- Switch to pnpm workspaces and use \"workspace:\" specifiers for local packages. Translate catalog: to pnpm.overrides (or npm overrides) to pin/fork transitive deps repo‑wide. ([pnpm.io](https://pnpm.io/9.x/workspaces?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you want, I can:\n- Draft a minimal sea-config.json set and the esbuild config you can drop in.\n- Provide a GitHub Actions matrix for both tracks (SEA embed vs. lazy download).\n- Generate a codemod to swap your Bun.embeddedFiles logic to node:sea, and provide the first‑run downloader with checksums."
            }
