              "text": "Short answer\n- skipLibCheck doesn’t block module augmentation. It only skips type-checking .d.ts files; the files are still read and merged. ([typescriptlang.org](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/skipLibCheck.html?utm_source=openai))\n- What’s really happening is that TypeScript isn’t “seeing” the augmentation .d.ts that adds .openapi(). With moduleResolution: \"Bundler\", subpath imports must expose their own types via package.json \"exports\". Many packages don’t ship a \"types\" target for subpaths like \"zod-openapi/extend\", so tsc resolves the JS but never loads the matching declaration that contains declare module 'zod'. No augmentation loaded → property 'openapi' doesn’t exist. ([typescriptdocs.com](https://typescriptdocs.com/modules-reference/reference.html?utm_source=openai))\n- Yes, a local ambient .d.ts that re-declares the augmentation is a safe, common fix. Keep the runtime call to extend the prototype, but teach the compiler about it locally.\n\nPractical fixes (pick one)\n\nOption A — Add a local augmentation shim (most robust)\n1) Keep your runtime extend (one of):\n- import 'zod-openapi/extend'  // runtime side effects\nor\n- import { extendZodWithOpenApi } from 'zod-openapi'; extendZodWithOpenApi(z);\n\n2) Add src/types/zod-openapi-augment.d.ts:\n```ts\n// Ensure 'zod' is the module you import from everywhere (see note below).\nimport 'zod';\n\ndeclare module 'zod' {\n  interface ZodType {\n    openapi<T extends import('zod').ZodTypeAny>(\n      this: T,\n      metadata: import('zod-openapi').ZodOpenApiMetadata<T>\n    ): T;\n  }\n}\n```\nThis file is ambient and gets picked up by your existing include. Because it only augments types, it won’t change runtime behavior. This works regardless of skipLibCheck. ([typescriptlang.org](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/declaration-merging.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nOption B — Point the subpath to the correct .d.ts using paths\nIf you want to keep import 'zod-openapi/extend' as the single source of truth:\n- Add to tsconfig.json:\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"baseUrl\": \".\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"zod-openapi/extend\": [\n        \"node_modules/zod-openapi/dist/extendZodTypes.d.ts\"\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\nTypeScript will now load the augmentation from that file when you import the subpath; your bundler/runtime still uses the JS file. This relies on an internal path in the package, so it’s a little more brittle if the library rearranges files, but it avoids maintaining a local declaration.\n\nOption C — Don’t use the subpath; call the function and rely on the root types\nSome packages import their augmentation .d.ts from the root index.d.ts. If that’s the case in your version, simply:\n```ts\nimport { z } from 'zod';\nimport { extendZodWithOpenApi } from 'zod-openapi';\nextendZodWithOpenApi(z);\n```\nand remove the subpath import. If your version doesn’t pull in the augmentation via the root types, fall back to A or B.\n\nAnswers to your specific questions\n1) Does skipLibCheck: true prevent module augmentation from being loaded from node_modules .d.ts files?\n- No. It only stops type-checking those files; it doesn’t stop them from being read/merged. ([typescriptlang.org](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/skipLibCheck.html?utm_source=openai))\n\n2) What’s the correct way to make TypeScript recognize module augmentation from a package when using skipLibCheck: true?\n- Ensure the augmentation declaration file is included in the type program. With moduleResolution: \"Bundler\", that often means:\n  - Import something whose .d.ts actually references the augmentation, or\n  - Add a tsconfig paths mapping to the augmentation .d.ts (Option B), or\n  - Add a local ambient declaration that re-declares the augmentation (Option A).\nThe presence of skipLibCheck is irrelevant to whether it loads; it only affects whether errors inside that .d.ts are reported. ([typescriptdocs.com](https://typescriptdocs.com/modules-reference/reference.html?utm_source=openai))\n\n3) Should I copy the declare module 'zod' block into a local .d.ts file instead of importing it?\n- Yes, that’s a perfectly fine solution and is often the most stable across tooling combinations. Keep the runtime initializer (extendZodWithOpenApi or the extend subpath import) so the method actually exists at runtime, and keep the ambient .d.ts so the compiler knows about it.\n\nGotchas to double‑check\n- Make sure you import from the same module you’re augmenting. If you import z from 'zod/v4', then an augmentation that says declare module 'zod' won’t apply; you’d need to also augment 'zod/v4'. Mismatched module specifiers are a very common reason augmentations “don’t work.” ([github.com](https://github.com/asteasolutions/zod-to-openapi/issues/340?utm_source=openai))\n- To verify what TypeScript is including, run: npx tsc --explainFiles or npx tsc --traceResolution and look for the augmentation file.\n\nWhy the subpath import is flaky under bundler resolution\n- When moduleResolution is \"Bundler\", TypeScript follows package.json \"exports\" strictly. If the package doesn’t provide a \"types\" condition for a subpath (e.g., \"./extend\"), tsc resolves the JS for runtime but never sees the corresponding .d.ts, so the augmentation isn’t loaded. That’s why Option B (paths) or a local shim (Option A) fixes it. ([typescriptdocs.com](https://typescriptdocs.com/modules-reference/reference.html?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you want, paste the exact zod-openapi version and I can suggest the smallest change that works for that version with your current tsconfig."
