E**\nOur `Storage.readJSON<T>()` function:\n```typescript\nexport async function readJSON<T>(key: string) {\n  return file(path.join(dir, key + \".json\"))\n    .text()\n    .then((t) => JSON.parse(t)) as Promise<T>\n}\n```\n\nWith Bun, `Bun.file().json()` returned the generic type. With Node.js, `JSON.parse()` correctly returns `unknown`, and TypeScript propagates this:\n```typescript\nconst read = await Storage.readJSON<MessageV2.Info>(p)  // read is unknown\nresult.push({ info: read, parts: await parts(sessionID, read.id) })  \n// Error: 'read.id' is of type 'unknown'\n```\n\nThis cascades to ~71 errors throughout the codebase.\n\n**Q:** What's the best practice for typing JSON deserialization in strict TypeScript without adding runtime validation to every call site? We want type safety but minimal changes. Options:\n- Type assertion at the generic function level (unsafe but practical)?\n- Different generic constraint approach?\n- Something with `as unknown as T`?\n\n---\n\n### 4. Property access on `{}` type (22 errors)\nObjects typed via zod schemas lose their inferred types somewhere:\n```typescript\n// cfg is typed from zod schema with experimental.hook.file_edited\ncfg.experimental?.hook?.file_edited  // Error: Property 'hook' does not exist on type '{}'\n\n// v1.metadata.assistant typed via zod\nv1.metadata.assistant!.cost  // Error: Property 'cost' does not exist on type '{}'\n```\n\nThe zod schemas ARE properly defined, but type inference collapses to `{}`.\n\n**Q:** What commonly causes zod-inferred types to collapse to `{}`? Is this a tsconfig/moduleResolution issue, or something about how `z.infer<>` works with optional/nested objects?\n\n---\n\n**Goal:** Identify the root causes and suggest minimal fixes that will resolve the bulk of these errors. We prefer fixing the source (e.g., one function definition, one type) over patching dozens of call sites.",
