              "text": "Short answer\n- These aren’t “pnpm resolution” bugs. They’re declaration-emit/naming issues that happen when an exported function’s inferred return type includes third‑party classes or types that TypeScript can’t give a stable public name to (especially with pnpm’s virtual store paths). \n- The 80/20 fix is to give every exported function an explicit, named return type that only references public, top‑level exports (or your own local aliases), not anonymous classes or deep/internal types. \n- If you don’t need .d.ts files from this package, turn off declaration emit and the errors go away.\n\nWhat to do first (fastest path to green checks)\n1) If this package does not need to emit .d.ts:\n- In its tsconfig:\n  {\n    \"compilerOptions\": {\n      \"declaration\": false,\n      \"emitDeclarationOnly\": false,\n      \"composite\": false,   // if not using project references here\n      \"skipLibCheck\": true\n    }\n  }\n- Rebuild. TS2742/TS4058/TS4094 typically only surface when TS has to produce declarations.\n\n2) If you do need declarations (library or project references):\n- Add explicit return types that refer only to public, named types (yours or top‑level package exports). Avoid exposing concrete class instance types (which carry private/protected fields) and avoid deep import paths.\n\nConcrete patches\n\nA) mcp/index.ts – clients\nProblem: Returning a Record of concrete MCP Client instances causes:\n- TS2742 (cannot be named without .pnpm path)\n- TS4094 (anonymous class type has private/protected)\n\nFix: Widen to an interface you own (only the public surface you use), and annotate the return. Cast once at the boundary.\n\nExample:\n```ts\n// Pick the minimal public surface you actually use.\nexport interface McpClientLike {\n  listTools(): Promise<unknown>;\n  callTool(name: string, input: unknown): Promise<unknown>;\n  close(): void | Promise<void>;\n}\n\nexport type ClientsMap = Record<string, McpClientLike>;\n\nexport async function clients(): Promise<ClientsMap> {\n  const s = await state();\n  return s.clients as unknown as ClientsMap;\n}\n```\nNotes:\n- If you have a public type from the MCP package (e.g., import type { Client } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk'), prefer that instead of inventing McpClientLike. Just don’t expose the concrete class with private fields.\n\nB) provider/provider.ts – tools\nProblem: Inferred return type mentions Metadata from another module, but the name isn’t available to the emitter.\n\nFix 1 (preferred): Import the names and annotate the return.\n```ts\nimport type { Tool, Metadata } from '../tool/tool'; // ensure these are exported from that module\n\nexport async function tools(providerID: string): Promise<readonly Tool<Metadata>[]> {\n  return (TOOL_MAPPING[providerID] ?? TOOLS) as readonly Tool<Metadata>[];\n}\n```\n\nFix 2 (if you don’t want to import generics): Define a local alias that doesn’t mention Metadata at the top level.\n```ts\ntype ToolDef = import('../tool/tool').Tool<import('../tool/tool').Metadata>;\n\nexport async function tools(providerID: string): Promise<readonly ToolDef[]> {\n  return (TOOL_MAPPING[providerID] ?? TOOLS) as readonly ToolDef[];\n}\n```\n\nFix 3 (pure 80/20): If you only need it to typecheck, widen metadata to unknown so the name doesn’t leak.\n```ts\ntype BasicTool = { id: string; run(input: unknown): Promise<unknown>; metadata?: unknown };\nexport async function tools(providerID: string): Promise<readonly BasicTool[]> {\n  return TOOL_MAPPING[providerID] ?? TOOLS as unknown as readonly BasicTool[];\n}\n```\n\nC) server/server.ts – openapi\nProblem: Inferred return type points at openapi-types via a .pnpm internal path.\n\nFix: Import a public name and annotate the return.\n```ts\nimport type { OpenAPIV3_1 } from 'openapi-types';\n\nexport async function openapi(): Promise<OpenAPIV3_1.Document> {\n  const a = app();\n  const result = await generateSpecs(a, { /* ... */ });\n  return result as OpenAPIV3_1.Document;\n}\n```\n\ntsconfig knobs that help\n- skipLibCheck: true\n  - Won’t fix your own file errors, but reduces noise from dependencies.\n- declaration / composite\n  - If you can, disable for apps. Most of these errors appear because TS is trying to write .d.ts.\n- verbatimModuleSyntax: true\n  - Keeps type-only imports as imports; helps keep names available without pulling in values.\n- noEmit: true\n  - If you only run tsc for typechecking, this avoids emit entirely, but if declarations are on, TS still needs to be able to name exported types. Combine with declaration: false if possible.\n\nWhy this happens (so you can spot it next time)\n- When an exported function’s type is inferred and that inference includes:\n  - private/protected members from a class (TS4094), or\n  - types that are only reachable via deep/internal module paths (TS2742), or\n  - types that aren’t in scope as named imports (TS4058),\n  the declaration emitter refuses to bake those unstable paths into your .d.ts. pnpm’s virtual store makes those paths look especially ugly, but it’s just a symptom; the real fix is to give TS a stable, named type.\n\nIf you want the absolute minimum changes to pass typecheck today\n- Add explicit return types that widen away from the problematic types:\n```ts\n// mcp/index.ts\nexport async function clients(): Promise<Record<string, unknown>> { ... }\n\n// provider/provider.ts\nexport async function tools(providerID: string): Promise<readonly unknown[]> { ... }\n\n// server/server.ts\nexport async function openapi(): Promise<unknown> { ... }\n```\n- Then tighten these back up later to proper named types.\n\nIf you share a bit of the actual types of your MCP client, Tool, and what generateSpecs returns, I can give you exact signatures that won’t leak private fields and won’t trip the emitter."
